-- Poi — points of interest as a TEMPLATE rather than as hand-written code.
--
-- The arch was written twice and got the wrong shape both times, because each
-- kind was its own algorithm arrived at by trying it, looking at a screenshot,
-- and trying again. This module is the answer to that: a kind is a row in a
-- table plus a shape built out of shared profiles, and the placement, the local
-- frame, the invariants and the preview come with it.
--
-- ── The one distinction that decides everything ─────────────────────────────
--
-- A kind is a SCATTER or a LANDMARK, and almost every other choice follows.
--
--   scatter   many, small, on a small cell grid. Its rules are read at the
--             COLUMN being evaluated, because a site probe would run for nine
--             cells on every column and no small-cell kind can afford that. It
--             therefore fades at the edges of its country rather than stopping,
--             and its shapes are allowed to be clipped. Buttes are a scatter.
--
--   landmark  few, large, on a big cell grid. Its rules are read at the SITE,
--             once, so every column agrees about whether it exists and how big
--             it is -- which is what lets it be carved as well as raised. It is
--             whole or absent, never clipped. The arch is a landmark, and so
--             are the crystal cavern, the giant tree and the atoll.
--
-- ── The two passes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
--   height    `height_at`, called from `Noise.height` per column. This is what
--             makes a feature read as TERRAIN: surface materials, strata,
--             talus, trees, the lake pour and the biome all follow from it. A
--             feature that should look like ground goes here, never in a stamp.
--
--   volume    `carve`, called per chunk after the fill. Only what a heightmap
--             cannot express: a hole, a cavern, an overhang.
--
-- ── The rules (`rule_ok`, checked for every kind by one test) ───────────────
--
--   1. reach <= cell, so two instances of a kind never overlap and a column
--      need only look at the 3x3 around it.
--   2. an opening must be strictly inside the mass that carries it: twice its
--      half-width under the flat top, and a band of at least `roof()` above it.
--      An arch whose opening is as tall as its rise is a lintel, not an arch --
--      this is the rule that would have caught the first one.
--   3. a level top comes from an ABSOLUTE top level, never from a rise added
--      to the ground, which tilts with the slope under it.
--   4. a vertical wall uses `prof_hard`; anything meant to blend uses
--      `prof_flat` or `prof_round`. A one-block ramp is neither and leaves a
--      half-height shelf.
--   5. a landmark's site rules never read `height` (no bottom to that
--      recursion); `height_base` is safe and is what they use.
--
-- ── The preview (`CF_POI_PREVIEW=<kind>`) ───────────────────────────────────
--
-- Prints the kind's table and three orthographic sections of it standing on
-- flat ground -- along the frame, across it, and in plan -- so a shape is read
-- in a second instead of hunted for in a world with a blind camera. Every
-- iteration on a shape should happen here.
mod CubeForge.Poi do

  needs IO.Process   -- CF_POI, CF_POI_DENSITY
  needs IO.Console   -- CF_POI_PREVIEW
  needs IO.Foreign   -- the beam kernels in the shim (cf_beam_min_sd, cf_beam_grid)
  needs Window

  alias CubeForge.Noise as N
  alias CubeForge.Chunk as C

  -- ── Kinds ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  fn k_butte() : Int do 0 end
  fn k_arch() : Int do 1 end
  fn k_cavern() : Int do 2 end
  fn k_tree() : Int do 3 end
  fn k_canyon() : Int do 4 end
  fn k_atoll() : Int do 5 end
  fn k_delta() : Int do 6 end
  doc "How many kinds there are: the rules test walks 0 .. kinds() - 1."
  fn kinds() : Int do 7 end
  fn kind_name(k : Int) : String do
    if k == k_butte() do "butte" else if k == k_arch() do "arch" else if k == k_cavern() do "cavern"
    else if k == k_tree() do "giant tree" else if k == k_canyon() do "canyon" else if k == k_atoll() do "atoll" else "delta" end end end end end end
  end

  -- ── What a kind does to the world ────────────────────────────────────────
  -- Orthogonal to scatter/landmark: a kind may raise the ground, may write
  -- into the volume, or both. The arch does both; the cavern and the giant
  -- tree only write volume, and a butte only raises.

  fn vol_none() : Int do 0 end
  doc "Removes ground: an opening, a chamber."
  fn vol_carve() : Int do 1 end
  doc "Writes blocks into air: a trunk, a canopy."
  fn vol_stamp() : Int do 2 end
  doc "What kind [k] does to the volume."
  fn vol_of(k : Int) : Int do
    if k == k_butte() || k == k_atoll() || k == k_canyon() || k == k_delta() do vol_none()
    else if k == k_tree() do vol_stamp()
    else vol_carve() end end
  end
  doc """
  Does the kind contribute to `height`. A feature that should read as TERRAIN
  must -- that is what gets it surface materials, strata, talus and trees. A
  feature that is not terrain (a cavern in the rock, a tree standing on it)
  must not, and is kept out of the per-column scan entirely.
  """
  fn raises(k : Int) : Bool do k == k_butte() || k == k_arch() || k == k_canyon() || k == k_atoll() || k == k_delta() end

  fn cls_scatter() : Int do 0 end
  fn cls_landmark() : Int do 1 end
  doc """
  A FIELD has no cells and no site at all: it is a term evaluated at every
  column from what `height` already has in hand -- the river, its crease, the
  ruggedness -- gated by a country mask. A canyon is not a thing placed in a
  valley, it is what the valley becomes where canyon country is; so it is a
  field. Fields skip every cell rule and cannot be carved.
  """
  fn cls_field() : Int do 2 end
  doc "Scatter, landmark or field: see the module header. This decides where a kind's rules are read and whether it may be carved."
  fn class_of(k : Int) : Int do
    if k == k_butte() do cls_scatter()
    else if k == k_canyon() || k == k_delta() do cls_field()
    else cls_landmark() end end
  end

  doc "The least rock a carved opening keeps over it. Rule 2."
  fn roof() : Int do 8 end

  -- ── The placement table ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

  doc "Side of the kind's cell in blocks: one instance at most per cell."
  fn cell(k : Int) : Int do
    if k == k_butte() do 24 else if k == k_cavern() do 128 else if k == k_atoll() do 256 else if class_of(k) == cls_field() do 1 else 160 end end end end
  end
  doc "Fraction of cells in full country holding one."
  fn density(k : Int) : Float do
    if k == k_butte() do 0.35 else if k == k_arch() do 0.5 else if k == k_cavern() do 0.65
    else if k == k_atoll() do 0.85 else if class_of(k) == cls_field() do 1.0 else 0.4 end end end end end
  end
  doc "Frequency of the kind's country octave: how wide a region of them is."
  fn cty_freq(k : Int) : Float do
    if k == k_butte() do 0.0075 else if k == k_arch() do 0.0035 else if k == k_cavern() do 0.0040
    -- a canyon SYSTEM runs for hundreds of blocks, so its country is the broadest octave here
    else if k == k_canyon() do 0.0025 else if k == k_atoll() do 0.0030
    -- a great river is a regional thing: the broadest octave of all
    else if k == k_delta() do 0.0020 else 0.0060 end end end end end end
  end
  doc "Where the country octave has to reach before the kind appears at all: its rarity."
  fn cty_lo(k : Int) : Float do
    if k == k_butte() do 0.62 else if k == k_arch() do 0.56 else if k == k_cavern() do 0.52
    else if k == k_canyon() do 0.66 else if k == k_atoll() do 0.38 else if k == k_delta() do 0.74 else 0.60 end end end end end end
  end
  doc "How wide the country's edge band is."
  fn cty_band(_k : Int) : Float do 0.10 end
  doc "How far outside a chunk an instance may sit and still write into it."
  fn reach(k : Int) : Int do
    if k == k_butte() do 12 else if k == k_arch() do 26 else if k == k_cavern() do 54
    else if k == k_atoll() do 90 else if class_of(k) == cls_field() do 0 else 58 end end end end end
  end
  doc "The least a landmark's ground must stand above sea level."
  fn min_ground(k : Int) : Int do
    if k == k_butte() do 0
    else if k == k_arch() do N.sea_level() + 5
    -- A cavern needs rock over it across its whole footprint, not just at its
    -- site (see `cover`). That makes this threshold do far more work than it
    -- looks: at sea + 26 it held over a fifty-block cross and no world in forty
    -- seeds had a cavern at all.
    else if k == k_cavern() do N.sea_level() + 18
    -- an atoll's ground rule is a CEILING, not a floor: see max_ground
    else if k == k_atoll() do 0
    else if class_of(k) == cls_field() do 0
    else N.sea_level() + 3 end end end end end
  end
  doc """
  The most the ground may stand over a landmark's footprint. An atoll wants to
  START under water, and no more than that: its lagoon is cut to an absolute
  floor and its ring raised to an absolute top, so a shallow shelf serves as
  well as a deep sea. It had asked for eight under the sea across the ring, and
  this terrain has shelf rather than ocean -- the placement gate print showed
  the ground at hashed sites at 49..105 over thirty seeds, the shallowest
  barely wet. Every other kind has no ceiling.
  """
  fn max_ground(k : Int) : Int do if k == k_atoll() do N.sea_level() - 6 else 9999 end end

  -- ── The parameter table ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  -- Six numbers per kind. A kind that does not use one leaves it zero.

  doc "Parameter 0: half-extent along the frame's axis."
  fn p_len() : Int do 0 end
  doc "Parameter 1: half-extent across the frame."
  fn p_wide() : Int do 1 end
  doc "Parameter 2: blocks added above the ground."
  fn p_rise() : Int do 2 end
  doc "Parameter 3: an ABSOLUTE top level, for a shape whose top must be flat whatever the ground does (rule 3)."
  fn p_top() : Int do 3 end
  doc "Parameter 4: half-width of the opening, along the frame."
  fn p_hole_w() : Int do 4 end
  doc "Parameter 5: height of the opening above its floor."
  fn p_hole_h() : Int do 5 end
  doc "Parameter 6: how far below the surface the shape sits. Underground kinds only."
  fn p_depth() : Int do 6 end
  fn params() : Int do 7 end

  fn plo(k : Int, i : Int) : Int do
    if k == k_butte() do (if i == p_len() do 5 else if i == p_wide() do 5 else if i == p_top() do 74 else 0 end end end)
    else if k == k_arch() do (if i == p_len() do 24 else if i == p_wide() do 5 else if i == p_rise() do 26 else 0 end end end)
    -- cavern: len is how far its chambers spread, wide is a chamber's radius
    else if k == k_cavern() do (if i == p_len() do 16 else if i == p_wide() do 12 else if i == p_depth() do 30 else 0 end end end)
    -- atoll: len is the ring's radius, wide half its width, top the reef flat, depth the lagoon floor below the sea
    else if k == k_atoll() do (if i == p_len() do 26 else if i == p_wide() do 5 else if i == p_top() do N.sea_level() + 1 else if i == p_depth() do 22 else 0 end end end end)
    else if class_of(k) == cls_field() do 0
    -- tree: len is the crown's radius, wide the trunk's, rise the trunk's height
    else (if i == p_len() do 26 else if i == p_wide() do 4 else if i == p_rise() do 14 else 0 end end end) end end end end end
  end
  fn phi(k : Int, i : Int) : Int do
    if k == k_butte() do (if i == p_len() do 11 else if i == p_wide() do 11 else if i == p_top() do 100 else 0 end end end)
    else if k == k_arch() do (if i == p_len() do 36 else if i == p_wide() do 8 else if i == p_rise() do 40 else 0 end end end)
    else if k == k_cavern() do (if i == p_len() do 26 else if i == p_wide() do 20 else if i == p_depth() do 44 else 0 end end end)
    else if k == k_atoll() do (if i == p_len() do 44 else if i == p_wide() do 9 else if i == p_top() do N.sea_level() + 3 else if i == p_depth() do 30 else 0 end end end end)
    else if class_of(k) == cls_field() do 0
    else (if i == p_len() do 38 else if i == p_wide() do 6 else if i == p_rise() do 22 else 0 end end end) end end end end end
  end

  pfn hash(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, salt : Int) : Float do
    N.hash2(cx * 7919 + salt * 104729, cz * 6271 + salt * 15485863, seed + 2100 + 200 * k)
  end

  doc """
  Parameter [i] of the instance in cell (cx, cz): a hash in [lo, hi], except
  where a kind DERIVES one from another. The derived ones exist so rule 2 holds
  by construction rather than by luck -- an opening cannot be made wider than
  the mass over it by turning a knob.
  """
  fn param(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, i : Int) : Int do
    if k == k_arch() && i == p_hole_w() do param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_len()) * 45 / 100
    else if k == k_arch() && i == p_hole_h() do param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_rise()) - roof() - 2
    else
      let lo = plo(k, i)
      let hi = phi(k, i)
      if hi <= lo do lo else lo + float_to_int(hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 10 + i) *. int_to_float(hi - lo + 1)) end
    end end
  end

  -- Buttes are round, so their two half-extents are one number.
  fn radius(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int) : Int do param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_len()) end

  -- ── The frame ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  -- Every kind has a site column, the ground under it, and a bearing; a shape
  -- is written in (u along, v across) so it never has to think about world axes.

  pfn floor_div(a : Int, b : Int) : Int do if a >= 0 do a / b else (a - b + 1) / b end end

  fn sx(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int) : Int do cx * cell(k) + float_to_int(hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 2) *. int_to_float(cell(k))) end
  fn sz(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int) : Int do cz * cell(k) + float_to_int(hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 3) *. int_to_float(cell(k))) end
  doc "Which way the instance faces, in radians."
  fn bearing(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int) : Float do hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 4) *. 6.2831853 end
  doc "Offset of (wx, wz) along the instance's axis."
  fn u_of(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, wx : Int, wz : Int) : Float do
    let a = bearing(k, cx, cz, seed)
    int_to_float(wx - sx(k, cx, cz, seed)) *. Math.cos(a) +. int_to_float(wz - sz(k, cx, cz, seed)) *. Math.sin(a)
  end
  doc "Offset of (wx, wz) across it."
  fn v_of(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, wx : Int, wz : Int) : Float do
    int_to_float(wz - sz(k, cx, cz, seed)) *. Math.cos(bearing(k, cx, cz, seed))
      -. int_to_float(wx - sx(k, cx, cz, seed)) *. Math.sin(bearing(k, cx, cz, seed))
  end

  doc "The kind's country mask at (wx, wz): one broad octave over a high threshold."
  fn country(k : Int, wx : Int, wz : Int, seed : Int) : Float do
    -- an atoll's country IS the ocean's: giving it an octave of its own meant
    -- the two rarely coincided, and a ring probed thirty blocks out from a deep
    -- site kept finding the coast of the ocean's own patch. One in 140 seeds.
    if k == k_atoll() do N.ocean_mask(int_to_float(wx), int_to_float(wz), seed)
    else N.smooth(N.clamp01((N.value2(int_to_float(wx) *. cty_freq(k), int_to_float(wz) *. cty_freq(k), seed + 2301 + 200 * k) -. cty_lo(k)) /. cty_band(k))) end
  end

  -- ── Profiles ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  -- Rule 4: a shape picks one of these; there is no fifth, ad-hoc option.

  doc "A cliff: 1 inside [r], 0 outside, with no ramp at all. What a butte wall and a mesa rim are."
  fn prof_hard(d : Float, r : Float) : Float do if d >= r do 0.0 else 1.0 end end
  doc "Flat over the middle, easing to nothing over the outer [ease] of the radius. What a ridge's length is."
  fn prof_flat(d : Float, r : Float, ease : Float) : Float do N.smooth(N.clamp01((1.0 -. d /. r) /. ease)) end
  doc "Rounded from the middle out. What a ridge's thickness is, so its top is walkable rather than a knife edge."
  fn prof_round(d : Float, r : Float) : Float do N.smooth(N.clamp01((1.0 -. d /. r) *. 1.6)) end

  -- ── Placement ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  doc "Are points of interest on at all: CF_POI=0 turns every one off."
  fn poi_on() : Bool do Env.get_int("CF_POI", 1) != 0 end
  doc "Scales every density: CF_POI_DENSITY percent, 100 by default; 0 with CF_POI=0."
  fn poi_scale() : Float do if !poi_on() do 0.0 else int_to_float(Env.get_int("CF_POI_DENSITY", 100)) /. 100.0 end end

  doc """
  Does cell (cx, cz) hold an instance of [k]. A LANDMARK reads its country and
  its ground at the SITE, so the height pass and the carve pass cannot disagree
  (rule 5: `height_base`, never `height`). A SCATTER cannot afford a site probe
  -- nine small cells on every column -- so its country is folded into [scale]
  by the caller, read at the column.
  """
  fn here(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, scale : Float) : Bool do
    if class_of(k) == cls_scatter() do hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 1) <= density(k) *. scale
    else
      if hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 1) > density(k) *. scale *. country(k, sx(k, cx, cz, seed), sz(k, cx, cz, seed), seed) do false
      else cover(k, cx, cz, seed) >= min_ground(k) && cover_hi(k, cx, cz, seed) <= max_ground(k) end
    end
  end

  doc "The HIGHEST ground over the instance's footprint: what an atoll is checked against, because its ring must lie in open water all the way round. Only the atoll probes; everything else asks its own column."
  fn cover_hi(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int) : Int do
    let x = sx(k, cx, cz, seed)
    let z = sz(k, cx, cz, seed)
    let at = N.height_base(x, z, seed)
    if k != k_atoll() do at
    else
      -- probed a little inside the ring's radius: it is the ring and the lagoon
      -- that must lie in water, and the outer foot may climb onto a shelf.
      -- At the full radius no seed in a hundred and forty had an atoll.
      let r = param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_len()) * 3 / 4
      Math.max_int(Math.max_int(at, Math.max_int(N.height_base(x + r, z, seed), N.height_base(x - r, z, seed))),
                   Math.max_int(N.height_base(x, z + r, seed), N.height_base(x, z - r, seed)))
    end
  end

  doc """
  The lowest ground over the instance's whole footprint, not just at its site.
  A cavern spreads its chambers thirty blocks sideways, and the site's own
  column says nothing about the hillside they run into: checking the site alone
  put chambers through the side of a hill, where the sea promptly filled them.
  Only a landmark that reaches wide needs the probes; everything else asks its
  own column.
  """
  fn cover(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int) : Int do
    let x = sx(k, cx, cz, seed)
    let z = sz(k, cx, cz, seed)
    let at = N.height_base(x, z, seed)
    if k != k_cavern() do at
    else
      -- the chambers sit within p_len of the site, so that is the span that has
      -- to be covered; probing out to len + wide as well rejected every world
      let r = param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_len())
      Math.min_int(Math.min_int(at, Math.min_int(N.height_base(x + r, z, seed), N.height_base(x - r, z, seed))),
                   Math.min_int(N.height_base(x, z + r, seed), N.height_base(x, z - r, seed)))
    end
  end

  doc "The ground an instance stands on: height_base at its site, which carries no POI term. A cavern hangs off its cover instead, so a chamber under a slope follows the low side rather than the high one."
  fn ground(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int) : Int do
    if k == k_cavern() do cover(k, cx, cz, seed) else N.height_base(sx(k, cx, cz, seed), sz(k, cx, cz, seed), seed) end
  end

  -- ── The height pass ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  doc """
  Blocks cell (cx, cz)'s instance would add at (wx, wz) if it existed, given the
  column's height with dunes [hd]: the SHAPE alone, with no placement in it. One
  case per kind, built from the shared profiles.

  Shape and placement are separated because the preview draws the shape and
  nothing else -- a preview that asked whether an instance happened to be placed
  at the sample cell would usually draw an empty sky, which is exactly what the
  first run of it did.
  """
  fn shape_at(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, wx : Int, wz : Int, seed : Int, hd : Float) : Int do
    if !raises(k) do 0
    else if class_of(k) == cls_field() do 0
    else if k == k_butte() do
      -- a mesa: a hard rim (rule 4) and an ABSOLUTE top (rule 3), so its top is
      -- level whatever the ground under it does, and a cell whose ground is
      -- already near its top holds nothing
      let dx = wx - sx(k, cx, cz, seed)
      let dz = wz - sz(k, cx, cz, seed)
      let r = int_to_float(radius(k, cx, cz, seed))
      if prof_hard(Math.sqrt(int_to_float(dx * dx + dz * dz)), r) <= 0.0 do 0
      else
        let rise = param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_top()) - float_to_int(hd)
        if rise < roof() do 0 else rise end
      end
    else if k == k_atoll() do atoll_at(k, cx, cz, wx, wz, seed, hd)
    else
      -- a fin: flat-topped along its length, rounded across it
      let fu = prof_flat(Math.abs(u_of(k, cx, cz, seed, wx, wz)), int_to_float(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_len())), 0.5)
      let fv = prof_round(Math.abs(v_of(k, cx, cz, seed, wx, wz)), int_to_float(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_wide())))
      float_to_int(int_to_float(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_rise())) *. fu *. fv)
    end end end end
  end

  -- ── The atoll ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  -- A reef ring in open ocean round a deep lagoon. Both the ring's top and the
  -- lagoon's floor are ABSOLUTE levels (rule 3): the sea bed under an atoll is
  -- whatever it is, and a reef flat that followed it would not be flat.
  --
  -- One gap in the ring is forced below sea level, so the lagoon is always
  -- connected to the sea and fills by the sea rule rather than by the lake
  -- pour -- which is cut at tile seams and would leave a dry lagoon on one.

  doc "Half the angular width of the gap in the ring, radians."
  fn atoll_gap() : Float do 0.32 end
  doc "Over how many blocks the lagoon floor eases up to the ring's inner foot, and the ring's outer foot eases down to the moat."
  fn atoll_ease() : Float do 7.0 end
  doc "How far under the sea the water outside the ring is cut to, so the reef stands in water whatever the shelf was."
  fn atoll_moat() : Int do 6 end
  doc "Over how many blocks the moat eases back up to the bed it was cut from."
  fn atoll_apron() : Float do 18.0 end

  -- The ring's radius at bearing [ang]: the hashed radius wobbled by a low
  -- octave, so it is not a compass circle.
  pfn atoll_r(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, wx : Int, wz : Int) : Float do
    int_to_float(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_len()))
      *. (1.0 +. 0.16 *. (N.value2(int_to_float(wx) *. 0.03, int_to_float(wz) *. 0.03, seed + 2500 + cx * 7 + cz * 13) -. 0.5) *. 2.0)
  end

  pfn atoll_at(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, wx : Int, wz : Int, seed : Int, hd : Float) : Int do
    let dx = int_to_float(wx - sx(k, cx, cz, seed))
    let dz = int_to_float(wz - sz(k, cx, cz, seed))
    let d = Math.sqrt(dx *. dx +. dz *. dz)
    let r = atoll_r(k, cx, cz, seed, wx, wz)
    let w = int_to_float(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_wide()))
    let top = int_to_float(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_top()))
    let floor = int_to_float(N.sea_level() - param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_depth()))
    let ease = atoll_ease()
    -- the gap: the arc about the hashed bearing where the ring is not raised
    let ang = Math.atan2(dz, dx)
    let gap_c = bearing(k, cx, cz, seed)
    let da0 = Math.abs(ang -. gap_c)
    let da = if da0 > 3.14159 do 6.2831853 -. da0 else da0 end
    let in_gap = da < atoll_gap()
    -- the apron: a moat cut to atoll_moat() round the outside of the ring,
    -- easing back to the bed over atoll_apron(). This terrain has shelf, not
    -- ocean: without it the ring stood in two blocks of water and read as a
    -- sandbar round a pit, not as a reef in the sea.
    let moat = int_to_float(N.sea_level() - atoll_moat())
    if d > r +. w +. ease +. atoll_apron() do 0
    else if d > r +. w +. ease do
      let t = N.smooth((d -. (r +. w +. ease)) /. atoll_apron())
      let want = moat +. (hd -. moat) *. t
      if want < hd do float_to_int(want -. hd) else 0 end
    else if d > r +. w do
      -- the outer foot: from the ring's top down to the moat
      let t = N.smooth((d -. r -. w) /. ease)
      let want = if in_gap do (if moat < hd do moat else hd end) else top +. (moat -. top) *. t end
      float_to_int(want -. hd)
    else if d >= r -. w do
      -- the ring itself, a little higher in the middle; in the gap, the channel
      if in_gap do (if moat < hd do float_to_int(moat -. hd) else 0 end)
      else float_to_int(top +. (if Math.abs(d -. r) < w *. 0.4 do 1.0 else 0.0 end) -. hd) end
    else if d >= r -. w -. ease do
      -- the inner foot: from the lagoon floor up to the ring -- or, in the
      -- gap, up to the moat, so the channel out to sea is a ramp a boat could
      -- take and not a thirty-block wall where the lagoon meets the shelf
      let t = N.smooth((d -. (r -. w -. ease)) /. ease)
      let want = if in_gap do floor +. (moat -. floor) *. t else floor +. (top -. floor) *. t end
      float_to_int(want -. hd)
    else float_to_int(floor -. hd) end end end end end
  end

  -- The shape, once placement has agreed there is one here.
  pfn rise_at(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, wx : Int, wz : Int, seed : Int, scale : Float, hd : Float) : Int do
    let dx = wx - sx(k, cx, cz, seed)
    let dz = wz - sz(k, cx, cz, seed)
    let far = param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_len())
    -- cheap rejection before the trig and before the site probe, which is what
    -- keeps nine cells a column affordable
    if dx > far || dx < 0 - far || dz > far || dz < 0 - far do 0
    else if !here(k, cx, cz, seed, scale) do 0
    else shape_at(k, cx, cz, wx, wz, seed, hd) end end
  end

  -- ── The canyon: a field ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  -- Where canyon country is, the river valley is re-cut as a SLOT: the river
  -- mask is sharpened so the walls stand near-vertical instead of a V, the cut
  -- is dropped by canyon_depth, and it is quantised to benches so the walls
  -- step down. The floor keeps clear of the sea, so the channel is a brook and
  -- not an arm of the ocean, and the existing valley-spring rule waters it.

  doc "How deep a canyon is cut below the valley it replaces: 40 young, 60 ancient."
  fn canyon_depth(a : Float) : Float do 40.0 +. 20.0 *. a end
  doc "Bench height, blocks: the walls step down in these."
  fn canyon_bench() : Int do 6 end
  doc "The least a column must stand above the sea before canyon country cuts it."
  fn canyon_rise_above() : Int do 20 end

  doc "Blocks the canyon term removes at a column: a negative number, or 0. [cty] is the canyon country there -- the caller reads it, so the preview can hand in 1 and draw the shape without a placement in it."
  fn canyon_at(seed : Int, hd : Float, river : Float, rug : Float, cty0 : Float) : Int do
    let cty = strength(cty0)
    let above = hd -. int_to_float(N.sea_level() + canyon_rise_above())
    if above <= 0.0 do 0
    else if river <= 0.05 do 0
    else if rug < 0.3 do 0
    else
      if cty <= 0.0 do 0
      else
        -- the slot: 0 at the valley's lip, 1 across most of its floor
        let slot = N.smooth(N.clamp01((river -. 0.15) /. 0.45))
        let want = canyon_depth(N.age_frac(seed)) *. slot *. cty *. N.smooth(N.clamp01((rug -. 0.3) /. 0.3)) *. poi_scale()
        -- benches, and never below the brook's floor
        let cut = float_to_int(want) / canyon_bench() * canyon_bench()
        let most = float_to_int(above) + canyon_rise_above() - 4
        0 - (if cut > most do most else cut end)
      end
    end end end
  end

  -- ── The delta: a field ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  -- Where great-river country meets the coast, two things happen to the
  -- river. Inland, the TRUNK: the raw crease is read against a far lower edge
  -- than the ordinary river uses, so the channel is 20-40 across instead of 3,
  -- and its bed is cut below the sea so it holds water its whole length. At
  -- the mouth, the FAN: the lobe is flattened to a block above the sea and a
  -- braid of channels cut across it, and the sediment pass already puts mud
  -- and clay under a floodplain, so the land between reads as delta unasked.
  --
  -- The honest limit, from the design: with no flow routing the great river is
  -- great only where its country is, and fades to an ordinary river upstream.

  doc """
  Where the trunk's channel starts on the raw crease, against the ordinary
  river's 0.66..0.86. The crease is a RIDGED field, 1 - |2n - 1|, so it is
  above 0.6 wherever the noise is within 0.2 of a half -- a band, not a line:
  at 0.55 the trunk was forty-five columns across, at 0.60 up to seventy-eight.
  0.72 is a channel twenty to forty wide, which is what a great river is.
  """
  fn delta_edge() : Float do 0.72 end
  doc "The highest ground the delta touches: above this it is not the coast."
  fn delta_rise_above() : Int do 16 end
  doc "How far below the sea the trunk's bed is cut at the channel's centre."
  fn delta_bed() : Float do 4.0 end

  doc """
  A country mask as a field should use it: full strength over nearly all of
  the country and easing only at the very edge. Scaling a cut by the raw mask
  gave half-dug channels -- a delta's trunk at seed 13 read as one bench along
  its whole length, a dry trough with its bed still above the sea, because the
  mask there was 0.4.
  """
  fn strength(cty : Float) : Float do N.smooth(N.clamp01(cty *. 2.5)) end

  doc "Blocks the delta term adds or removes at a column: [rv] is the raw crease, [cty0] the great-river country there. The preview hands in 1."
  fn delta_at(seed : Int, wx : Int, wz : Int, hd : Float, rv : Float, cty0 : Float) : Int do
    let sea = int_to_float(N.sea_level())
    let cty = strength(cty0)
    if hd > sea +. int_to_float(delta_rise_above()) do 0
    else if cty <= 0.0 do 0
    else
      -- 1 at the mouth (at or under the sea), 0 six blocks up
      let coast = N.smooth(N.clamp01((sea +. 6.0 -. hd) /. 8.0))
      let wide = N.smooth(N.clamp01((rv -. delta_edge()) /. (1.0 -. delta_edge())))
      -- the fan is the MOUTH of this channel and nothing else: it is gated on
      -- the trunk's own width. Gated on the raw crease it flattened every
      -- coastal column in great-river country -- 11,000 of a window's 16,384
      -- at seed 13, fifty-eight columns of seabed raised into land in one row
      -- -- because the crease is high across half the world.
      let lobe = wide
      if coast < 0.2 do
        -- the trunk: cut the wide channel's bed below the sea
        let bed = sea -. delta_bed() *. wide
        if wide <= 0.0 || bed >= hd do 0 else float_to_int((bed -. hd) *. cty) end
      else if lobe <= 0.0 do 0
      else
        -- the fan: flatten the lobe to a block above the sea, and braid it
        let flat = (sea +. 1.0 -. hd) *. 0.85 *. coast *. lobe
        let braid = N.ridged(int_to_float(wx) *. 0.045 +. 1.7, int_to_float(wz) *. 0.045 +. 4.1, seed + 2600)
        let channel = if braid > 0.80 do ((sea -. 2.0) -. (hd +. flat)) *. lobe else 0.0 end
        float_to_int((flat +. channel) *. cty)
      end end
    end end
  end

  pfn pick(best : Int, cand : Int) : Int do
    if best == 0 do cand else if cand == 0 do best else Math.max_int(best, cand) end end
  end

  pfn kind_go(k : Int, wx : Int, wz : Int, seed : Int, scale : Float, hd : Float, i : Int, best : Int) : Int do
    if i >= 9 do best
    else
      let cx = floor_div(wx, cell(k)) - 1 + i % 3
      let cz = floor_div(wz, cell(k)) - 1 + i / 3
      -- max, not sum: two overlapping instances are one mass at the taller of
      -- them, never a tower of both their rises. But a CUT must beat nothing:
      -- an atoll's lagoon is negative, and against a best of zero `max` threw
      -- it away -- the site said -12 and the world said 0. An instance never
      -- overlaps another of its kind (reach <= cell), so "nonzero beats zero,
      -- then max" is exact.
      kind_go(k, wx, wz, seed, scale, hd, i + 1, pick(best, rise_at(k, cx, cz, wx, wz, seed, scale, hd)))
    end
  end

  doc """
  Blocks every landform point of interest adds at (wx, wz) -- or removes, for a
  canyon. [dmask] is the dune mask, [hd] the column's height with dunes,
  [river] and [rv] the river mask and its raw crease, [rug] the ruggedness: all
  already computed by `height` for its own purposes, so a field costs nothing
  to gate.

  Outside a kind's country this is zero and costs one broad octave -- the nine
  cell hashes and the CF_POI read are both behind that gate, so a column in open
  country pays almost nothing.
  """
  fn height_at(wx : Int, wz : Int, seed : Int, dmask : Float, hd : Float, river : Float, rv : Float, rug : Float) : Int do
    -- the butte: a scatter, so its country and its desert mask are read here,
    -- at the column, and folded into the density
    let b = if dmask <= 0.0 do 0
            else
              let cty = country(k_butte(), wx, wz, seed)
              -- doubled before clamping, so the inside of a field is at full
              -- density and only its fringe thins; a field that faded linearly
              -- from its centre never reads as a field at all
              if cty <= 0.0 do 0 else kind_go(k_butte(), wx, wz, seed, N.clamp01(2.0 *. cty *. dmask) *. poi_scale(), hd, 0, 0) end
            end
    -- the arch: a landmark, so the country here only GATES the scan; whether it
    -- exists was settled at its site. The mask still multiplies the fin, so
    -- where the gate cuts, the fin has already faded to nothing
    let cty = country(k_arch(), wx, wz, seed)
    let a = if cty <= 0.0 do 0 else float_to_int(int_to_float(kind_go(k_arch(), wx, wz, seed, poi_scale(), hd, 0, 0)) *. cty) end
    -- the atoll: a landmark in the sea, so the scan is gated on the column
    -- being no higher than its reef flat -- the ring's outer parts stand on
    -- shelf that may be nearly dry
    let at = if hd > int_to_float(N.sea_level() + 3) do 0
             else
               let cty2 = country(k_atoll(), wx, wz, seed)
               if cty2 <= 0.0 do 0 else kind_go(k_atoll(), wx, wz, seed, poi_scale(), hd, 0, 0) end
             end
    -- the raised kinds take the max; the canyon is a cut and adds to that
    -- (a canyon through a butte field cuts the mesas too, which is right)
    -- the canyon is a field: its country is read here and the cut is added
    -- to whatever was raised (a canyon through a butte field cuts the mesas
    -- too, which is right). The octave is behind the same cheap gates as the
    -- cut itself, so open lowland never samples it.
    let cut = if hd < int_to_float(N.sea_level() + canyon_rise_above()) || river <= 0.05 || rug < 0.3 do 0
              else canyon_at(seed, hd, river, rug, country(k_canyon(), wx, wz, seed)) end
    -- the atoll lowers as well as raises, and nothing else raises under the
    -- sea, so where it speaks it is the answer
    -- the delta, a field too, disjoint from the canyon by height: a canyon
    -- wants sea + 20 and above, a delta sea + 16 and below
    let dl = if hd > int_to_float(N.sea_level() + delta_rise_above()) || rv <= 0.45 do 0
             else delta_at(seed, wx, wz, hd, rv, country(k_delta(), wx, wz, seed)) end
    (if at != 0 do at else Math.max_int(b, a) end) + cut + dl
  end

  -- ── The volume pass ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  --
  -- ONE function decides what a kind does to a voxel, and both the generator
  -- and the preview call it. That is deliberate: the preview exists to be
  -- believed, and a preview that modelled the shape separately would drift from
  -- what the world actually builds -- which is the whole class of bug this
  -- module was written to end.
  --
  --   -1  leave the voxel alone
  --    0  clear it (only ever ground: never water, never bedrock)
  --   id  write this block into it, if it is air

  doc "May the carve take this block: anything but air, water and bedrock. A river under an arch stays a river."
  fn carvable(id : Int) : Bool do
    if id == 0 do false else if id == C.bedrock() do false else !C.is_water(id) end end
  end

  pfn sq(a : Float) : Float do a *. a end

  -- Position along the segment (ax, ay, az)-(ex, ey, ez) nearest (px, py, pz),
  -- clamped to 0..1. What a taper is written against.
  pfn seg_t(px : Float, py : Float, pz : Float, ax : Float, ay : Float, az : Float, ex : Float, ey : Float, ez : Float) : Float do
    let dx = ex -. ax
    let dy = ey -. ay
    let dz = ez -. az
    let len2 = dx *. dx +. dy *. dy +. dz *. dz
    if len2 <= 0.0001 do 0.0 else N.clamp01(((px -. ax) *. dx +. (py -. ay) *. dy +. (pz -. az) *. dz) /. len2) end
  end

  doc """
  Distance from (px, py, pz) to the axis (ax, ay, az)-(ex, ey, ez), measured in
  a PRISM's norm rather than a circle's: an octagonal cross-section, so a beam
  has flat faces and hard edges.

  This is the thing that was wrong with the crystals, and no amount of tuning
  their length or count was going to fix it. A radial distance gives a cylinder,
  and a voxelised cylinder is a staircase — round in principle, lumpy in fact.
  A selenite beam is a prism: big flat planes meeting at sharp edges, which is
  most of what makes one read as a crystal rather than as a pale rock.

  The cross-section basis is free: for an axis `d`, `e1 = (dz, 0, -dx)` is
  perpendicular to it by inspection, and the second component follows from
  Pythagoras against the full perpendicular offset, so no second basis vector
  and no cross product are needed.
  """
  pfn prism_d(px : Float, py : Float, pz : Float, ax : Float, ay : Float, az : Float, ex : Float, ey : Float, ez : Float) : Float do
    let dx = ex -. ax
    let dy = ey -. ay
    let dz = ez -. az
    let t = seg_t(px, py, pz, ax, ay, az, ex, ey, ez)
    -- offset from the nearest point on the axis
    let ox = px -. (ax +. dx *. t)
    let oy = py -. (ay +. dy *. t)
    let oz = pz -. (az +. dz *. t)
    let r2 = ox *. ox +. oy *. oy +. oz *. oz
    let h = Math.sqrt(dx *. dx +. dz *. dz)
    -- a near-vertical beam has no horizontal component to build e1 from
    let a = if h < 0.0001 do ox else (ox *. dz -. oz *. dx) /. h end
    let b2 = r2 -. a *. a
    let b = if b2 <= 0.0 do 0.0 else Math.sqrt(b2) end
    let fa = Math.abs(a)
    -- an octagon, not a square: four faces read as a crate, eight as a crystal
    Math.max_float(Math.max_float(fa, b), (fa +. b) *. 0.7071)
  end

  -- Squared distance from (px, py, pz) to the segment (ax, ay, az)-(ex, ey, ez).
  -- The end point is `e`, not `b`: `by` is a keyword, and a parameter named
  -- that is a parse error on the signature line with no hint as to why.
  pfn seg_d2(px : Float, py : Float, pz : Float, ax : Float, ay : Float, az : Float, ex : Float, ey : Float, ez : Float) : Float do
    let dx = ex -. ax
    let dy = ey -. ay
    let dz = ez -. az
    let len2 = dx *. dx +. dy *. dy +. dz *. dz
    let t0 = if len2 <= 0.0001 do 0.0 else ((px -. ax) *. dx +. (py -. ay) *. dy +. (pz -. az) *. dz) /. len2 end
    let t = N.clamp01(t0)
    sq(px -. (ax +. dx *. t)) +. sq(py -. (ay +. dy *. t)) +. sq(pz -. (az +. dz *. t))
  end

  -- ── The arch's opening ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  pfn vol_arch(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, wx : Int, wz : Int, y : Int, g : Int) : Int do
    let u = u_of(k, cx, cz, seed, wx, wz)
    let hw = int_to_float(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_hole_w()))
    let q = 1.0 -. sq(u /. hw)
    -- through the whole fin and a little past it, so the opening is a hole
    -- rather than a pair of alcoves
    if Math.abs(v_of(k, cx, cz, seed, wx, wz)) > int_to_float(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_wide())) +. 2.0 do 0 - 1
    else if q <= 0.0 do 0 - 1
    else if y < g + 1 do 0 - 1
    else if y > g + 1 + float_to_int(int_to_float(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_hole_h())) *. Math.sqrt(q)) do 0 - 1
    else 0 end end end end
  end

  -- ── The cavern's chambers ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  doc "How many chambers a cavern has."
  fn chambers() : Int do 3 end

  doc "Chamber [j]'s centre, in the frame, and its radius. Public so the CF_TERRAIN_STATS line can say where to stand to see one."
  fn cham_u(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int) : Float do
    (hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 40 + 3 * j) -. 0.5) *. 2.0 *. int_to_float(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_len()))
  end
  fn cham_v(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int) : Float do
    (hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 41 + 3 * j) -. 0.5) *. 2.0 *. int_to_float(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_len()))
  end
  doc "Chamber [j]'s centre in world x / z: cham_u / cham_v through the instance's bearing."
  fn cham_wx(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int) : Int do
    float_to_int(uv_wx(k, cx, cz, seed, cham_u(k, cx, cz, seed, j), cham_v(k, cx, cz, seed, j)))
  end
  fn cham_wz(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int) : Int do
    float_to_int(uv_wz(k, cx, cz, seed, cham_u(k, cx, cz, seed, j), cham_v(k, cx, cz, seed, j)))
  end
  fn cham_y(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, g : Int) : Float do
    int_to_float(g - param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_depth())) +. (hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 42 + 3 * j) -. 0.5) *. 10.0
  end
  -- The multiplier never exceeds 1, so `phi(p_wide)` is a true maximum and the
  -- roof rule can be written against it. It used to reach 1.25, which put a
  -- chamber's ceiling above the rock the rule had reserved for it: at the
  -- largest radius the caverns broke the surface and flooded.
  fn cham_r(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int) : Float do
    int_to_float(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_wide())) *. (0.62 +. 0.38 *. hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 60 + j))
  end

  -- The nearest chamber's surface, as a fraction of its radius: under 1 is
  -- inside it. Chambers are joined by a tube along the line between them, so a
  -- cavern is one place rather than three unconnected bubbles.
  pfn cav_d(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, u : Float, v : Float, y : Float, g : Int, j : Int, best : Float) : Float do
    if j >= chambers() do best
    else
      let r = cham_r(k, cx, cz, seed, j)
      let d = Math.sqrt(sq(u -. cham_u(k, cx, cz, seed, j)) +. sq(v -. cham_v(k, cx, cz, seed, j)) +. sq(y -. cham_y(k, cx, cz, seed, j, g))) /. r
      -- the tube to the next chamber, at a third of the radius
      let n = (j + 1) % chambers()
      let td = Math.sqrt(seg_d2(u, y, v, cham_u(k, cx, cz, seed, j), cham_y(k, cx, cz, seed, j, g), cham_v(k, cx, cz, seed, j),
                                cham_u(k, cx, cz, seed, n), cham_y(k, cx, cz, seed, n, g), cham_v(k, cx, cz, seed, n))) /. (r *. 0.34)
      cav_d(k, cx, cz, seed, u, v, y, g, j + 1, Math.min_float(best, Math.min_float(d, td)))
    end
  end

  -- ── Selenite beams ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  -- A model blade cannot be longer than the cell it is drawn in, so the LONG
  -- crystals are geometry, not models: a beam is a segment through the chamber,
  -- the same primitive the giant tree's boughs use, and the block it is made of
  -- greedy-meshes into a prism. Naica's are metres across and span the cave.

  -- Salt ranges, kept apart on purpose: the direction, length and radius draws
  -- take 110..193 (four per beam, twenty-one beams), the three centre offsets
  -- 200..220, 230..250 and 260..280, and the size class 290..310. They collided
  -- once when the beam count changed, and a collision here is invisible -- two
  -- draws that should be independent quietly agree.
  doc "Beams crossing each chamber."
  -- Sixteen a chamber. Naica is packed: the beams are most of what you see,
  -- and at ten the room still read as a room with some beams in it.
  fn beams() : Int do beams_free() + beams_anchored() end
  doc "Beams crossing the chamber's air: centred anywhere in it, free hashed directions."
  fn beams_free() : Int do 12 end
  doc """
  Beams growing OUT OF the shell: anchored at a hashed point on the wall or
  floor (the anchor's elevation is biased downward, so three in four stand on
  the floor) and pointing inward with a hashed lean, one end buried in the
  rock. Naica's floor is a field of these; without them the floor and walls
  of a chamber were bare and every beam hung overhead.
  """
  fn beams_anchored() : Int do 14 end
  fn beam_anchored(b : Int) : Bool do b >= beams_free() end
  pfn anch_th(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Float do hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 300 + 7 * j + b) *. 6.2831853 end
  -- elevation of the anchor from -1.45 (the floor) to +0.35: the hash is
  -- squared toward the floor, so half of them stand within 30 degrees of it
  pfn anch_ph(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Float do
    let h = hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 330 + 7 * j + b)
    0.0 -. 1.45 +. h *. h *. 1.8
  end
  pfn anch_nx(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Float do Math.cos(anch_ph(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b)) *. Math.cos(anch_th(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b)) end
  pfn anch_ny(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Float do Math.sin(anch_ph(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b)) end
  pfn anch_nz(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Float do Math.cos(anch_ph(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b)) *. Math.sin(anch_th(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b)) end
  -- the axis: inward (minus the anchor's direction) with a lean of up to 0.6 on each axis, normalised
  pfn anch_jx(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Float do 0.0 -. anch_nx(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) +. (hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 360 + 7 * j + b) -. 0.5) *. 1.2 end
  pfn anch_jy(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Float do 0.0 -. anch_ny(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) +. (hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 390 + 7 * j + b) -. 0.5) *. 1.2 end
  pfn anch_jz(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Float do 0.0 -. anch_nz(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) +. (hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 420 + 7 * j + b) -. 0.5) *. 1.2 end
  pfn anch_len(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Float do
    let x = anch_jx(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b)
    let y = anch_jy(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b)
    let z = anch_jz(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b)
    Math.max_float(0.0001, Math.sqrt(x *. x +. y *. y +. z *. z))
  end
  pfn anch_dx(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Float do anch_jx(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) /. anch_len(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) end
  pfn anch_dy(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Float do anch_jy(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) /. anch_len(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) end
  pfn anch_dz(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Float do anch_jz(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) /. anch_len(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) end
  -- Where beam [b] of chamber [j] is centred. A free beam anywhere in the
  -- chamber (0.9 of its radius each way, 0.7 vertically); an anchored one at
  -- its anchor, 1.05 radii out so an end is buried, plus half its length inward.
  pfn beam_ou(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Float do
    let r = cham_r(k, cx, cz, seed, j)
    if beam_anchored(b) do cham_u(k, cx, cz, seed, j) +. anch_nx(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) *. r *. 1.05 +. anch_dx(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) *. beam_half(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b)
    else cham_u(k, cx, cz, seed, j) +. (hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 200 + 7 * j + b) -. 0.5) *. 1.8 *. r end
  end
  pfn beam_ov(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Float do
    let r = cham_r(k, cx, cz, seed, j)
    if beam_anchored(b) do cham_v(k, cx, cz, seed, j) +. anch_nz(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) *. r *. 1.05 +. anch_dz(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) *. beam_half(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b)
    else cham_v(k, cx, cz, seed, j) +. (hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 230 + 7 * j + b) -. 0.5) *. 1.8 *. r end
  end
  pfn beam_oy(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int, g : Int) : Float do
    let r = cham_r(k, cx, cz, seed, j)
    if beam_anchored(b) do cham_y(k, cx, cz, seed, j, g) +. anch_ny(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) *. r *. 1.05 +. anch_dy(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) *. beam_half(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b)
    else cham_y(k, cx, cz, seed, j, g) +. (hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 260 + 7 * j + b) -. 0.5) *. 1.4 *. r end
  end

  -- Beam [b] of chamber [j]: a segment through the chamber at a hashed
  -- attitude, longer than the chamber is wide so it drives into both walls.
  -- Azimuth: fanned evenly round the compass, one slot per beam, with a hashed
  -- jitter -- the way the giant tree throws its boughs. With a free hash per
  -- beam, two of the six in a chamber lay nearly parallel through the same
  -- middle and merged into one slab; the cavern slice showed a single white
  -- mass 28 columns wide where the shafts were meant to cross.
  pfn beam_az(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Float do
    int_to_float(b) *. 6.2831853 /. int_to_float(beams()) +. (hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 110 + 4 * (7 * j + b)) -. 0.5) *. 0.6 +. int_to_float(j) *. 0.7
  end
  pfn beam_el(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Float do
    hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 111 + 4 * (7 * j + b)) *. 1.2 -. 0.6
  end
  pfn beam_dx(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Float do
    if beam_anchored(b) do anch_dx(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) else Math.cos(beam_az(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b)) *. Math.cos(beam_el(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b)) end
  end
  pfn beam_dy(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Float do
    if beam_anchored(b) do anch_dy(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) else Math.sin(beam_el(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b)) end
  end
  pfn beam_dz(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Float do
    if beam_anchored(b) do anch_dz(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) else Math.sin(beam_az(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b)) *. Math.cos(beam_el(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b)) end
  end
  doc """
  Is beam [b] one of the large ones. FOUR IN FIVE are: Naica is packed with big
  ones, and a chamber where the big shafts are the minority reads as a cave that
  happens to have some crystal in it rather than as a crystal cave. The rest are
  short stubs broken across them, which is what keeps it from reading as a
  lattice.
  """
  fn beam_long(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Bool do
    hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 290 + 7 * j + b) < 0.80
  end

  doc "Half the beam's length, in blocks. A long one overshoots the chamber's radius, so it is embedded in the rock at both ends; a short one is a stub a third of the way across."
  fn beam_half(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Float do
    let h = hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 112 + 4 * (7 * j + b))
    -- an anchored beam runs from the shell inward: a third to two thirds of the room
    if beam_anchored(b) do cham_r(k, cx, cz, seed, j) *. (0.18 +. 0.32 *. h)
    else if beam_long(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) do cham_r(k, cx, cz, seed, j) *. (1.10 +. 0.55 *. h)
    else cham_r(k, cx, cz, seed, j) *. (0.35 +. 0.30 *. h) end end
  end
  doc """
  The beam's radius at its thickest, in blocks. That thickness against its
  LENGTH is what makes it read as a crystal: at radius 2.5 the same beam looked
  like a pale boulder, because the eye reads thickness against length, not
  against the room it is in.
  """
  fn beam_rad(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int) : Float do
    let h = hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 113 + 4 * (7 * j + b))
    -- the long ones carry the look, so they are the medium-thick ones; the
    -- short ones stay slender or they read as boulders rather than crystals
    -- a tenth of the room's radius, as at Naica: a metre-thick crystal in a
    -- ten-metre chamber. At 2.0-3.3 in an eleven-radius chamber six of them
    -- were a third of the room each and fused into slabs
    -- an anchored beam is a stub from the floor or wall: slimmer than the
    -- beams that cross the room, or thirty of them fuse into a white floor
    if beam_anchored(b) do 0.55 +. 0.55 *. h
    else if beam_long(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) do 1.0 +. 0.6 *. h else 0.6 +. 0.4 *. h end end
  end
  doc """
  How thick the beam is at position [t] along it, as a fraction of beam_rad:
  FULL over the middle 72% and then a straight line to a point, a pyramid on
  the end of a prism. A smooth quartic taper made every beam a tusk: a curved
  outline, and a curved outline is the one thing a crystal never has. A
  selenite beam is a bar with flat sides and a chisel end.
  """
  fn beam_taper(t : Float) : Float do
    let e = Math.abs(2.0 *. t -. 1.0)
    if e <= 0.72 do 1.0 else (1.0 -. e) /. 0.28 end
  end


  doc "How far outside a beam's surface a block's centre may be and the block still be crystal_beam. The half-diagonal of a block is 0.87; a little under, so a block holding only a corner sliver is left out."
  fn beam_skin() : Float do 0.75 end

  -- ── The beam field: the beams at sub-block resolution ────────────────────
  doc """
  A beam is a prism one or two blocks across, and a block is one block across,
  so drawn as cubes a beam is a staircase of cubes, and every crystal picture
  in this branch's history was that staircase. So beam blocks are not drawn as
  cubes. The mesher asks for the cavern's beams as a table of segments
  (beam_table), cuts each beam block into 8x8x8 sub-cubes against the exact
  prisms (beam_grid), and stamps the result the way it stamps a mushroom. The
  block grid still holds crystal_beam wherever a beam passes -- for light,
  collision and the slice diagnostic -- and the LOOK comes from here.

  Eight floats per beam: both ends (x, y, z), the radius, one spare. The table
  is in the MESHER's coordinates (window-local), shifted y0 the caller's
  (wx0 - lx): the mesher never learns a world coordinate.
  """
  fn beam_stride() : Int do 8 end
  fn no_beams() : NativeF32Arr do NativeArray.make_f32(1, 0.0) end
  fn beam_count(t : NativeF32Arr) : Int do NativeArray.length_f32(t) / beam_stride() end

  -- (u, v) in the instance's frame back to world x / z: the inverse of u_of / v_of.
  pfn uv_wx(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, u : Float, v : Float) : Float do
    let a = bearing(k, cx, cz, seed)
    int_to_float(sx(k, cx, cz, seed)) +. u *. Math.cos(a) -. v *. Math.sin(a)
  end
  pfn uv_wz(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, u : Float, v : Float) : Float do
    let a = bearing(k, cx, cz, seed)
    int_to_float(sz(k, cx, cz, seed)) +. u *. Math.sin(a) +. v *. Math.cos(a)
  end
  -- One beam's eight floats at slot [i]. The centre and axis are exactly beam_d's.
  pfn beam_put(t : NativeF32Arr, i : Int, k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, b : Int, g : Int, shx : Float, shy : Float, shz : Float) : NativeF32Arr do
    let l = beam_half(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b)
    let ou = beam_ou(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b)
    let ov = beam_ov(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b)
    let oy = beam_oy(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b, g)
    let du = beam_dx(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) *. l
    let dy = beam_dy(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) *. l
    let dv = beam_dz(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b) *. l
    let o = i * beam_stride()
    t |> (fn a -> NativeArray.set_f32(a, o, uv_wx(k, cx, cz, seed, ou -. du, ov -. dv) -. shx))
      |> (fn a -> NativeArray.set_f32(a, o + 1, oy -. dy -. shy))
      |> (fn a -> NativeArray.set_f32(a, o + 2, uv_wz(k, cx, cz, seed, ou -. du, ov -. dv) -. shz))
      |> (fn a -> NativeArray.set_f32(a, o + 3, uv_wx(k, cx, cz, seed, ou +. du, ov +. dv) -. shx))
      |> (fn a -> NativeArray.set_f32(a, o + 4, oy +. dy -. shy))
      |> (fn a -> NativeArray.set_f32(a, o + 5, uv_wz(k, cx, cz, seed, ou +. du, ov +. dv) -. shz))
      |> (fn a -> NativeArray.set_f32(a, o + 6, beam_rad(k, cx, cz, seed, j, b)))
  end
  pfn beam_put_cell(t : NativeF32Arr, i : Int, k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, g : Int, shx : Float, shy : Float, shz : Float, jb : Int) : NativeF32Arr do
    if jb >= chambers() * beams() do t
    else beam_put_cell(beam_put(t, i + jb, k, cx, cz, seed, jb / beams(), jb % beams(), g, shx, shy, shz), i, k, cx, cz, seed, g, shx, shy, shz, jb + 1) end
  end
  doc """
  The beams of the cavern instance in cell (cx, cz), in the GENERATOR's frame
  (world coordinates, a block sampled at its integer corner): what vol_block
  reads, built once per instance per chunk rather than re-derived from the
  hashes at every voxel -- which cost a cavern chunk 137 ms against 2 ms for
  a plain one. no_beams() for any other kind.
  """
  fn cell_aux(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int) : NativeF32Arr do cell_aux_at(k, cx, cz, seed, ground(k, cx, cz, seed)) end
  doc "cell_aux for an instance standing on ground [g]: the preview draws every kind on flat ground."
  fn cell_aux_at(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, g : Int) : NativeF32Arr do
    if k != k_cavern() do no_beams()
    else beam_put_cell(NativeArray.make_f32(chambers() * beams() * beam_stride(), 0.0), 0, k, cx, cz, seed, g, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0) end
  end
  -- Does the cavern instance of cell (cx, cz) reach the chunk at (wx0, wz0)? apply_cell's test.
  pfn beam_cell_here(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, wx0 : Int, wz0 : Int, scale : Float) : Bool do
    if !here(k, cx, cz, seed, scale) do false
    else
      let dx = wx0 + 8 - sx(k, cx, cz, seed)
      let dz = wz0 + 8 - sz(k, cx, cz, seed)
      let r = reach(k) + 12
      !(dx > r || dx < 0 - r || dz > r || dz < 0 - r)
    end
  end
  -- Two passes over the cells the chunk can see: count the instances, then
  -- fill. The array is threaded through the fill (NativeArray.set_f32 returns
  -- the array to use from then on; a discarded result is a lost write).
  pfn beam_cells_count(k : Int, wx0 : Int, wz0 : Int, seed : Int, scale : Float, i : Int, cx0 : Int, cz0 : Int, nx : Int, nz : Int, n : Int) : Int do
    if i >= nx * nz do n
    else beam_cells_count(k, wx0, wz0, seed, scale, i + 1, cx0, cz0, nx, nz,
                          if beam_cell_here(k, cx0 + i % nx, cz0 + i / nx, seed, wx0, wz0, scale) do n + 1 else n end) end
  end
  pfn beam_cells_fill(t : NativeF32Arr, n : Int, k : Int, wx0 : Int, wz0 : Int, seed : Int, shx : Float, shz : Float, scale : Float,
                      i : Int, cx0 : Int, cz0 : Int, nx : Int, nz : Int) : NativeF32Arr do
    if i >= nx * nz do t
    else
      let cx = cx0 + i % nx
      let cz = cz0 + i / nx
      if !beam_cell_here(k, cx, cz, seed, wx0, wz0, scale) do beam_cells_fill(t, n, k, wx0, wz0, seed, shx, shz, scale, i + 1, cx0, cz0, nx, nz)
      else beam_cells_fill(beam_put_cell(t, n * chambers() * beams(), k, cx, cz, seed, ground(k, cx, cz, seed), shx, 0.0 -. 0.5, shz, 0),
                           n + 1, k, wx0, wz0, seed, shx, shz, scale, i + 1, cx0, cz0, nx, nz) end
    end
  end
  doc """
  Every cavern beam that can touch the chunk whose world origin is (wx0, wz0),
  in the MESHER's frame: a point at world (wx, y, wz) is at (wx - wx0 + lx, y,
  wz - wz0 + lz), plus a half on every axis. The generator samples a block at
  its integer corner and the mesher samples sub-cubes outward from the block's
  min corner, so the generator's point (wx, y, wz) IS the mesher's (wx + 0.5,
  y + 0.5, wz + 0.5); the half is folded into the shift here, once, so both
  see the same beam. Without it 148 of a chunk's 1008 beam blocks had no beam
  in them by the mesher's reckoning and were see-through holes. no_beams()
  when there is none, which is nearly every chunk.
  """
  fn beam_table(seed : Int, wx0 : Int, wz0 : Int, lx : Float, lz : Float) : NativeF32Arr do
    let scale = poi_scale()
    let k = k_cavern()
    if scale <= 0.0 do no_beams()
    else
      let r = reach(k)
      let a = cell(k)
      let cx0 = floor_div(wx0 - r, a)
      let cz0 = floor_div(wz0 - r, a)
      let nx = floor_div(wx0 + 15 + r, a) - cx0 + 1
      let nz = floor_div(wz0 + 15 + r, a) - cz0 + 1
      let shx = int_to_float(wx0) -. lx -. 0.5
      let shz = int_to_float(wz0) -. lz -. 0.5
      let n = beam_cells_count(k, wx0, wz0, seed, scale, 0, cx0, cz0, nx, nz, 0)
      if n == 0 do no_beams()
      else beam_cells_fill(NativeArray.make_f32(n * chambers() * beams() * beam_stride(), 0.0), 0, k, wx0, wz0, seed, shx, shz, scale, 0, cx0, cz0, nx, nz) end
    end
  end

  -- Signed distance from (x, y, z) to the surface of beam [i] of table [t], in blocks.
  pfn beam_sd(t : NativeF32Arr, i : Int, x : Float, y : Float, z : Float) : Float do
    let o = i * beam_stride()
    let ax = NativeArray.get_f32(t, o)
    let ay = NativeArray.get_f32(t, o + 1)
    let az = NativeArray.get_f32(t, o + 2)
    let ex = NativeArray.get_f32(t, o + 3)
    let ey = NativeArray.get_f32(t, o + 4)
    let ez = NativeArray.get_f32(t, o + 5)
    prism_d(x, y, z, ax, ay, az, ex, ey, ez) -. NativeArray.get_f32(t, o + 6) *. beam_taper(seg_t(x, y, z, ax, ay, az, ex, ey, ez))
  end
  pfn beam_inside_go(t : NativeF32Arr, n : Int, x : Float, y : Float, z : Float, i : Int) : Bool do
    if i >= n do false
    else if beam_sd(t, i, x, y, z) < 0.0 do true
    else beam_inside_go(t, n, x, y, z, i + 1) end end
  end
  pfn beam_min_go(t : NativeF32Arr, n : Int, x : Float, y : Float, z : Float, i : Int, best : Float) : Float do
    if i >= n do best else beam_min_go(t, n, x, y, z, i + 1, Math.min_float(best, beam_sd(t, i, x, y, z))) end
  end
  doc "beam_min_sd in March: the reference the shim's kernel is held to (poi_test)."
  fn beam_min_sd_ref(t : NativeF32Arr, x : Float, y : Float, z : Float) : Float do beam_min_go(t, beam_count(t), x, y, z, 0, 9.0) end
  doc """
  Signed distance, in blocks, from (x, y, z) to the nearest beam surface of
  [t]; 9 when the table is empty. The shim's kernel (cf_beam_min_sd): a cavern
  chunk asks this 864 thousand times, and at 320 ns a call in March that was
  the whole of its 132 ms.
  """
  fn beam_min_sd(t : NativeF32Arr, x : Float, y : Float, z : Float) : Float do CubeForge.Ffi.Window.beam_min_sd(t, x, y, z) end
  doc "Is the point (x, y, z), in the table's frame, inside any beam of [t]?"
  fn beam_inside(t : NativeF32Arr, x : Float, y : Float, z : Float) : Bool do beam_inside_go(t, beam_count(t), x, y, z, 0) end

  -- The beams of [t] whose surface comes within a block's half-diagonal of the
  -- centre of the block at (x0, y0, z0): the only ones its 512 sub-cubes need
  -- to ask. Two passes: count, then copy.
  pfn beam_near_count(t : NativeF32Arr, n : Int, x : Float, y : Float, z : Float, i : Int, m : Int) : Int do
    if i >= n do m
    else beam_near_count(t, n, x, y, z, i + 1, if beam_sd(t, i, x, y, z) < 0.9 do m + 1 else m end) end
  end
  pfn beam_near_fill(t : NativeF32Arr, n : Int, out : NativeF32Arr, m : Int, x : Float, y : Float, z : Float, i : Int) : NativeF32Arr do
    if i >= n do out
    else if beam_sd(t, i, x, y, z) >= 0.9 do beam_near_fill(t, n, out, m, x, y, z, i + 1)
    else beam_near_fill(t, n, copy8_go(t, i * beam_stride(), out, m * beam_stride(), 0), m + 1, x, y, z, i + 1) end end
  end
  pfn copy8_go(src : NativeF32Arr, si : Int, dst : NativeF32Arr, di : Int, j : Int) : NativeF32Arr do
    if j >= beam_stride() do dst else copy8_go(src, si, NativeArray.set_f32(dst, di + j, NativeArray.get_f32(src, si + j)), di, j + 1) end
  end
  doc "The beams of [t] that reach the block whose min corner is (x0, y0, z0): no_beams() when none does."
  fn beam_near(t : NativeF32Arr, x0 : Float, y0 : Float, z0 : Float) : NativeF32Arr do
    let n = beam_count(t)
    let m = beam_near_count(t, n, x0 +. 0.5, y0 +. 0.5, z0 +. 0.5, 0, 0)
    if m == 0 do no_beams()
    else beam_near_fill(t, n, NativeArray.make_f32(m * beam_stride(), 0.0), 0, x0 +. 0.5, y0 +. 0.5, z0 +. 0.5, 0) end
  end

  doc "The beam grid's side: 8 sub-cubes of the block and a margin of 1 on every side, the neighbouring blocks' nearest sub-cubes."
  fn beam_grid_n() : Int do 10 end
  -- Is grid cell coordinate [c] in the margin?
  pfn in_margin(c : Int) : Bool do c == 0 || c == beam_grid_n() - 1 end
  -- One beam's constants, hoisted once per block rather than read seven
  -- times per sub-cube: the segment (ax, ay, az) -> (ex, ey, ez), its
  -- squared length, the horizontal length that builds the cross-section
  -- basis, and the radius. The 896 sub-cubes that matter (the 8-cube plus
  -- the six face layers of the margin) are walked per beam.
  pfn beam_grid_beam(g : NativeU8Arr, x0 : Float, y0 : Float, z0 : Float,
                     ax : Float, ay : Float, az : Float, dx : Float, dy : Float, dz : Float, len2 : Float, h : Float, rad : Float, i : Int) : NativeU8Arr do
    let gn = beam_grid_n()
    if i >= gn * gn * gn do g
    else
      let x = i % gn
      let y = i / (gn * gn)
      let z = (i / gn) % gn
      let margins = (if in_margin(x) do 1 else 0 end) + (if in_margin(y) do 1 else 0 end) + (if in_margin(z) do 1 else 0 end)
      if margins >= 2 do beam_grid_beam(g, x0, y0, z0, ax, ay, az, dx, dy, dz, len2, h, rad, i + 1)
      else if NativeArray.get_u8(g, CubeForge.Model.idx_n(gn, x, y, z)) != 0 do beam_grid_beam(g, x0, y0, z0, ax, ay, az, dx, dy, dz, len2, h, rad, i + 1)
      else
        let px = x0 +. (int_to_float(x - 1) +. 0.5) /. 8.0
        let py = y0 +. (int_to_float(y - 1) +. 0.5) /. 8.0
        let pz = z0 +. (int_to_float(z - 1) +. 0.5) /. 8.0
        let t = N.clamp01(((px -. ax) *. dx +. (py -. ay) *. dy +. (pz -. az) *. dz) /. len2)
        let ox = px -. (ax +. dx *. t)
        let oy = py -. (ay +. dy *. t)
        let oz = pz -. (az +. dz *. t)
        let r2 = ox *. ox +. oy *. oy +. oz *. oz
        let fa = Math.abs(if h < 0.0001 do ox else (ox *. dz -. oz *. dx) /. h end)
        let b2 = r2 -. fa *. fa
        let fb = if b2 <= 0.0 do 0.0 else Math.sqrt(b2) end
        let d = Math.max_float(Math.max_float(fa, fb), (fa +. fb) *. 0.7071)
        beam_grid_beam(if d < rad *. beam_taper(t) do NativeArray.set_u8(g, CubeForge.Model.idx_n(gn, x, y, z), CubeForge.Model.c_crystal()) else g end,
                       x0, y0, z0, ax, ay, az, dx, dy, dz, len2, h, rad, i + 1)
      end end
    end
  end
  pfn beam_grid_go(g : NativeU8Arr, t : NativeF32Arr, n : Int, x0 : Float, y0 : Float, z0 : Float, bi : Int) : NativeU8Arr do
    if bi >= n do g
    else
      let o = bi * beam_stride()
      let ax = NativeArray.get_f32(t, o)
      let ay = NativeArray.get_f32(t, o + 1)
      let az = NativeArray.get_f32(t, o + 2)
      let dx = NativeArray.get_f32(t, o + 3) -. ax
      let dy = NativeArray.get_f32(t, o + 4) -. ay
      let dz = NativeArray.get_f32(t, o + 5) -. az
      let len2 = dx *. dx +. dy *. dy +. dz *. dz
      beam_grid_go(beam_grid_beam(g, x0, y0, z0, ax, ay, az, dx, dy, dz, if len2 <= 0.0001 do 0.0001 else len2 end, Math.sqrt(dx *. dx +. dz *. dz), NativeArray.get_f32(t, o + 6), 0),
                   t, n, x0, y0, z0, bi + 1)
    end
  end
  doc """
  The block at min corner (x0, y0, z0) cut into 8x8x8 sub-cubes against the
  beams of [t] (use beam_near's table), with a one-sub-cube margin from the
  blocks around it: a Model grid beam_grid_n() a side, selenite where a
  sub-cube's centre is inside a beam. Model.template_inner turns it into the
  block's faces, and only those the neighbours do not cover: without the
  margin a section of beams carried 1.6 million floats, most of them faces
  buried inside the beam between two of its own blocks. The cut is the
  shim's kernel (cf_beam_grid); beam_grid_ref is the same in March.
  """
  fn beam_grid(t : NativeF32Arr, x0 : Float, y0 : Float, z0 : Float) : NativeU8Arr do
    CubeForge.Ffi.Window.beam_grid(CubeForge.Model.empty_n(beam_grid_n()), t, x0, y0, z0, beam_grid_n(), CubeForge.Model.c_crystal())
  end
  doc "beam_grid in March: the reference the shim's kernel is held to (poi_test)."
  fn beam_grid_ref(t : NativeF32Arr, x0 : Float, y0 : Float, z0 : Float) : NativeU8Arr do
    beam_grid_go(CubeForge.Model.empty_n(beam_grid_n()), t, beam_count(t), x0, y0, z0, 0)
  end

  pfn vol_cavern(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, aux : NativeF32Arr, wx : Int, wz : Int, y : Int, g : Int) : Int do
    let u = u_of(k, cx, cz, seed, wx, wz)
    let v = v_of(k, cx, cz, seed, wx, wz)
    let d = cav_d(k, cx, cz, seed, u, v, int_to_float(y), g, 0, 9.0)
    -- a little past the shell, so a beam is driven into the rock at both ends
    -- rather than stopping neatly at the wall
    if d >= 1.45 do 0 - 1
    -- a block holds crystal_beam when the beam's surface passes within
    -- beam_skin of its centre: every block that holds any of the prism, so
    -- the mesher's sub-block cut (beam_grid) has somewhere to put each sliver
    else if beam_min_sd(aux, int_to_float(wx), int_to_float(y), int_to_float(wz)) < beam_skin() do C.crystal_beam()
    else if d >= 1.0 do 0 - 1
    -- No fine growth. The blade models on the shell read as white mushrooms
    -- stuck to the rock, first everywhere, then as floor rubble, and in every
    -- picture they were the thing the eye went to and the thing Naica has none
    -- of. Naica is the beams and the rock, nothing else. The blade block stays
    -- for the item and for anywhere else that wants a small crystal.
    else 0 end end end
  end

  -- ── The giant tree ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  -- A live oak, not a conifer: WIDER THAN IT IS TALL, on a short thick trunk
  -- that the branches leave low down. The first version was a 50-block pole
  -- with blobs round it, which is a different tree entirely -- the proportions
  -- are the whole character of this one, so they are the numbers to look at
  -- first in the preview.
  --
  -- A branch is two segments, not one: it sweeps out and slightly DOWN from the
  -- trunk, then lifts to its tip. That dip is what makes it read as a bough
  -- carrying its own weight rather than a spoke.

  doc "How many boughs a giant tree throws."
  fn branches() : Int do 7 end

  -- Where branch [j] leaves the trunk, how far it reaches, and how far it lifts.
  pfn br_h(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int) : Float do
    int_to_float(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_rise())) *. (0.55 +. 0.40 *. hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 70 + j))
  end
  pfn br_a(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int) : Float do
    bearing(k, cx, cz, seed) +. int_to_float(j) *. 6.2831853 /. int_to_float(branches()) +. (hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 80 + j) -. 0.5) *. 0.7
  end
  pfn br_len(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int) : Float do
    int_to_float(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_len())) *. (0.70 +. 0.40 *. hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 90 + j))
  end
  pfn br_lift(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int) : Float do
    br_len(k, cx, cz, seed, j) *. (0.15 +. 0.45 *. hash(k, cx, cz, seed, 100 + j))
  end
  pfn br_base_y(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, g : Int) : Float do int_to_float(g) +. br_h(k, cx, cz, seed, j) end
  pfn br_tip_u(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int) : Float do Math.cos(br_a(k, cx, cz, seed, j)) *. br_len(k, cx, cz, seed, j) end
  pfn br_tip_v(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int) : Float do Math.sin(br_a(k, cx, cz, seed, j)) *. br_len(k, cx, cz, seed, j) end
  pfn br_tip_y(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, g : Int) : Float do br_base_y(k, cx, cz, seed, j, g) +. br_lift(k, cx, cz, seed, j) end
  -- the elbow: half way out, dipped below the straight line
  pfn br_mid_u(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int) : Float do br_tip_u(k, cx, cz, seed, j) *. 0.5 end
  pfn br_mid_v(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int) : Float do br_tip_v(k, cx, cz, seed, j) *. 0.5 end
  pfn br_mid_y(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, j : Int, g : Int) : Float do
    br_base_y(k, cx, cz, seed, j, g) +. br_lift(k, cx, cz, seed, j) *. 0.5 -. br_len(k, cx, cz, seed, j) *. 0.16
  end

  -- Nearest bough, as a fraction of its own thickness. The inner half is
  -- thicker than the outer, so a bough tapers from the trunk to its tip.
  pfn br_d(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, u : Float, v : Float, y : Float, g : Int, j : Int, best : Float) : Float do
    if j >= branches() do best
    else
      let inner = Math.sqrt(seg_d2(u, y, v, 0.0, br_base_y(k, cx, cz, seed, j, g), 0.0,
                                   br_mid_u(k, cx, cz, seed, j), br_mid_y(k, cx, cz, seed, j, g), br_mid_v(k, cx, cz, seed, j))) /. 2.6
      let outer = Math.sqrt(seg_d2(u, y, v, br_mid_u(k, cx, cz, seed, j), br_mid_y(k, cx, cz, seed, j, g), br_mid_v(k, cx, cz, seed, j),
                                   br_tip_u(k, cx, cz, seed, j), br_tip_y(k, cx, cz, seed, j, g), br_tip_v(k, cx, cz, seed, j))) /. 1.8
      br_d(k, cx, cz, seed, u, v, y, g, j + 1, Math.min_float(best, Math.min_float(inner, outer)))
    end
  end

  -- A leaf mass at every bough's tip, so the canopy's edge follows the boughs
  -- rather than being a circle drawn over them.
  pfn leaf_d(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, u : Float, v : Float, y : Float, g : Int, j : Int, best : Float) : Float do
    if j >= branches() do best
    else
      let r = br_len(k, cx, cz, seed, j) *. 0.42
      let d = Math.sqrt(sq(u -. br_tip_u(k, cx, cz, seed, j)) +. sq(v -. br_tip_v(k, cx, cz, seed, j))
                        +. sq((y -. (br_tip_y(k, cx, cz, seed, j, g) +. r *. 0.35)) *. 1.6)) /. r
      leaf_d(k, cx, cz, seed, u, v, y, g, j + 1, Math.min_float(best, d))
    end
  end

  pfn vol_tree(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, wx : Int, wz : Int, y : Int, g : Int) : Int do
    let u = u_of(k, cx, cz, seed, wx, wz)
    let v = v_of(k, cx, cz, seed, wx, wz)
    let fy = int_to_float(y)
    let l = int_to_float(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_len()))
    let tr = int_to_float(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_wide()))
    let top = int_to_float(g) +. int_to_float(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_rise()))
    -- a short trunk that flares at the foot, like a live oak's buttress
    let trunk = if fy < int_to_float(g) || fy > top do false
                else Math.sqrt(u *. u +. v *. v) <= tr *. (1.35 -. 0.45 *. (fy -. int_to_float(g)) /. (top -. int_to_float(g))) end
    -- the crown: a broad flattened dome sitting ON the boughs, wider than the
    -- whole tree is tall
    let ch = int_to_float(g) +. int_to_float(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_rise())) +. l *. 0.42
    let cr = l *. 0.92
    let cd = Math.sqrt(u *. u +. v *. v +. sq((fy -. ch) *. 2.6)) /. cr
    if trunk do C.oak_log()
    else if br_d(k, cx, cz, seed, u, v, fy, g, 0, 9.0) < 1.0 do C.oak_log()
    else if leaf_d(k, cx, cz, seed, u, v, fy, g, 0, 9.0) < 1.0 do C.oak_leaves()
    else if cd < 1.0 do C.oak_leaves()
    else 0 - 1 end end end end
  end

  doc """
  What kind [k]'s instance in cell (cx, cz) does to the voxel (wx, y, wz), given
  the ground [g] its site stands on and [aux], the instance's cell_aux (built
  once per instance, not per voxel): -1 leave alone, 0 clear, or a block id to
  write into air. The generator and the preview both go through here.
  """
  fn vol_block(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, aux : NativeF32Arr, wx : Int, wz : Int, y : Int, g : Int) : Int do
    if k == k_arch() do vol_arch(k, cx, cz, seed, wx, wz, y, g)
    else if k == k_cavern() do vol_cavern(k, cx, cz, seed, aux, wx, wz, y, g)
    else if k == k_tree() do vol_tree(k, cx, cz, seed, wx, wz, y, g)
    else 0 - 1 end end end
  end

  doc "The lowest voxel kind [k] can touch, above the ground [g] its site stands on."
  fn vol_y0(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, g : Int) : Int do
    if k == k_cavern() do g - param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_depth()) - param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_wide()) - 8
    else g + 1 end
  end
  doc "The highest voxel kind [k] can touch."
  fn vol_y1(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, g : Int) : Int do
    if k == k_cavern() do g - param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_depth()) + param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_wide()) + 8
    else if k == k_tree() do g + param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_rise()) + param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_len())
    else g + 1 + param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_hole_h()) end end
  end

  -- ── Applying it to a chunk ───────────────────────────────────────────────

  pfn apply_y_go(c : CubeForge.Chunk.Chunk, k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, aux : NativeF32Arr,
                 wx : Int, wz : Int, x : Int, z : Int, y : Int, y1 : Int, g : Int) : CubeForge.Chunk.Chunk do
    if y > y1 do c
    else
      let c1 = if y < 1 || y >= 256 do c
               else
                 let b = vol_block(k, cx, cz, seed, aux, wx, wz, y, g)
                 let had = C.get(c, x, y, z)
                 if b < 0 do c
                 else if b == 0 do (if carvable(had) do C.set(c, x, y, z, 0) else c end)
                 -- A CARVE owns the volume it opens, so what it puts back --
                 -- the crystal lining a chamber's shell -- replaces the ground
                 -- it just took. A STAMP does not own anything: it writes only
                 -- into air, so a tree can never eat the hill it stands on.
                 -- Getting this the same for both left every cavern unlit: the
                 -- crystals were being asked to replace rock and declining.
                 else if vol_of(k) == vol_stamp() do (if had == 0 do C.set(c, x, y, z, b) else c end)
                 else (if carvable(had) || had == 0 do C.set(c, x, y, z, b) else c end) end end end
               end
      apply_y_go(c1, k, cx, cz, seed, aux, wx, wz, x, z, y + 1, y1, g)
    end
  end

  -- Every column of the chunk against one instance: 256 columns is cheaper to
  -- walk than the instance's own footprint, and it needs no clipping.
  pfn apply_col_go(c : CubeForge.Chunk.Chunk, k : Int, wx0 : Int, wz0 : Int, seed : Int, aux : NativeF32Arr,
                   cx : Int, cz : Int, i : Int, g : Int) : CubeForge.Chunk.Chunk do
    if i >= 256 do c
    else
      let x = i % 16
      let z = i / 16
      -- a column outside the instance's horizontal reach cannot be touched by
      -- it at any height, and the y walk is 60 voxels deep for a giant tree
      let dx = wx0 + x - sx(k, cx, cz, seed)
      let dz = wz0 + z - sz(k, cx, cz, seed)
      let r = reach(k)
      if dx * dx + dz * dz > r * r do apply_col_go(c, k, wx0, wz0, seed, aux, cx, cz, i + 1, g)
      else
        apply_col_go(apply_y_go(c, k, cx, cz, seed, aux, wx0 + x, wz0 + z, x, z,
                                vol_y0(k, cx, cz, seed, g), vol_y1(k, cx, cz, seed, g), g),
                     k, wx0, wz0, seed, aux, cx, cz, i + 1, g)
      end
    end
  end

  pfn apply_cell(c : CubeForge.Chunk.Chunk, k : Int, wx0 : Int, wz0 : Int, seed : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, scale : Float) : CubeForge.Chunk.Chunk do
    if !here(k, cx, cz, seed, scale) do c
    else
      -- a column of the chunk more than the instance's reach away cannot be
      -- touched by it, so a distant instance costs one distance test
      let dx = wx0 + 8 - sx(k, cx, cz, seed)
      let dz = wz0 + 8 - sz(k, cx, cz, seed)
      let r = reach(k) + 12
      if dx > r || dx < 0 - r || dz > r || dz < 0 - r do c
      else apply_col_go(c, k, wx0, wz0, seed, cell_aux(k, cx, cz, seed), cx, cz, 0, ground(k, cx, cz, seed)) end
    end
  end

  pfn apply_cells_go(c : CubeForge.Chunk.Chunk, k : Int, wx0 : Int, wz0 : Int, seed : Int, i : Int, cx0 : Int, cz0 : Int, nx : Int, nz : Int, scale : Float) : CubeForge.Chunk.Chunk do
    if i >= nx * nz do c
    else apply_cells_go(apply_cell(c, k, wx0, wz0, seed, cx0 + i % nx, cz0 + i / nx, scale), k, wx0, wz0, seed, i + 1, cx0, cz0, nx, nz, scale) end
  end

  pfn apply_kind(c : CubeForge.Chunk.Chunk, k : Int, wx0 : Int, wz0 : Int, seed : Int, scale : Float) : CubeForge.Chunk.Chunk do
    if vol_of(k) == vol_none() do c
    else
      let r = reach(k)
      let a = cell(k)
      let cx0 = floor_div(wx0 - r, a)
      let cz0 = floor_div(wz0 - r, a)
      -- the two spans are computed separately: at these cell sizes and 16-block
      -- chunks they are not always the same number
      apply_cells_go(c, k, wx0, wz0, seed, 0, cx0, cz0, floor_div(wx0 + 15 + r, a) - cx0 + 1, floor_div(wz0 + 15 + r, a) - cz0 + 1, scale)
    end
  end

  pfn apply_go(c : CubeForge.Chunk.Chunk, k : Int, wx0 : Int, wz0 : Int, seed : Int, scale : Float) : CubeForge.Chunk.Chunk do
    if k >= kinds() do c else apply_go(apply_kind(c, k, wx0, wz0, seed, scale), k + 1, wx0, wz0, seed, scale) end
  end

  doc "Write every point of interest's volume into the chunk at (wx0, wz0): the arch's opening, the cavern's chambers, the giant tree."
  fn carve(c : CubeForge.Chunk.Chunk, wx0 : Int, wz0 : Int, seed : Int) : CubeForge.Chunk.Chunk do
    let scale = poi_scale()
    if scale <= 0.0 do c else apply_go(c, 0, wx0, wz0, seed, scale) end
  end

  -- ── The rules ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  doc """
  The invariants of the template, for kind [k] at a sample cell. One test walks
  every kind through this, so a new kind cannot ship breaking them. See the
  module header for what each rule is for.
  """
  fn rule_ok(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int) : Bool do
    let len = param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_len())
    let hw = param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_hole_w())
    let hh = param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_hole_h())
    let rise = param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_rise())
    -- a field has no cells: only the rules about what it is apply
    if class_of(k) == cls_field() do raises(k) && vol_of(k) == vol_none()
    -- 1: an instance never leaves its own cell's reach
    else if reach(k) > cell(k) do false
    -- 1: the widest shape fits in the 3x3 a column looks at
    else if phi(k, p_len()) > cell(k) do false
    -- 2: an opening sits strictly inside the mass over it
    else if hw * 2 >= len do false
    else if hh > 0 && hh + roof() > rise do false
    -- 2, underground: a chamber keeps a roof's worth of rock over it, whatever
    -- its radius and however shallow it is allowed to sit
    -- the widest a chamber can be is phi(p_wide) exactly (see cham_r), and it
    -- must still keep a roof's worth of rock over it at the shallowest depth
    else if k == k_cavern() && plo(k, p_depth()) < phi(k, p_wide()) + roof() do false
    else if k == k_cavern() && min_ground(k) < plo(k, p_depth()) + phi(k, p_wide()) do false
    -- 3: a kind with a level top declares an absolute one
    else if k == k_butte() && phi(k, p_top()) <= 0 do false
    -- 3: an atoll's reef flat is an absolute level too
    else if k == k_atoll() && phi(k, p_top()) <= 0 do false
    -- 5: a landmark resolves at its site -- against a floor or a ceiling
    else if class_of(k) == cls_landmark() && min_ground(k) <= 0 && max_ground(k) >= 9999 do false
    -- a stamp is not terrain: it must not also raise the ground, or a tree
    -- would grow its own hill and get grass on the branches
    else if vol_of(k) == vol_stamp() && raises(k) do false
    else if vol_of(k) == vol_stamp() && phi(k, p_rise()) <= 0 do false
    -- a kind that neither raises nor writes volume does nothing at all
    else if !raises(k) && vol_of(k) == vol_none() do false
    else true end end end end end end end end end end end end end
  end

  -- ── The preview ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  -- Every iteration on a shape belongs here, not in a world with a blind
  -- camera. It draws through `shape_at` and `vol_block` -- the same two
  -- functions the generator uses -- so what it shows is what gets built.

  doc "Flat ground the preview stands kind [k] on: deep enough that an underground kind has rock over it."
  fn preview_ground(k : Int) : Int do
    if k == k_cavern() do min_ground(k) + 6
    else if k == k_atoll() do N.sea_level() - 12
    else if k == k_canyon() do N.sea_level() + 34
    else if k == k_delta() do N.sea_level() + 4
    else 70 end end end end
  end

  -- A field has no shape of its own to draw, so the preview hands it a valley:
  -- a river crease running along u, 22 blocks wide, in rugged country.
  pfn pv_field(k : Int, v : Int, g : Int) : Int do
    let river = N.smooth(N.clamp01(1.0 -. Math.abs(int_to_float(v)) /. 22.0))
    if k == k_canyon() do canyon_at(1, int_to_float(g), river, 0.7, 1.0)
    else if k == k_delta() do delta_at(1, v * 3, 0, int_to_float(g), N.clamp01(1.0 -. Math.abs(int_to_float(v)) /. 30.0), 1.0)
    else 0 end end
  end

  -- World column of frame offset (u, v).
  pfn pv_wx(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, u : Int, v : Int) : Int do
    sx(k, cx, cz, seed) + float_to_int(int_to_float(u) *. Math.cos(bearing(k, cx, cz, seed)) -. int_to_float(v) *. Math.sin(bearing(k, cx, cz, seed)))
  end
  pfn pv_wz(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, u : Int, v : Int) : Int do
    sz(k, cx, cz, seed) + float_to_int(int_to_float(u) *. Math.sin(bearing(k, cx, cz, seed)) +. int_to_float(v) *. Math.cos(bearing(k, cx, cz, seed)))
  end

  -- One character: what the generator would leave at (u, v, y) on flat ground.
  --   '#' ground   ':' the kind's own raised mass   '.' carved void
  --   '*' crystal  'T' trunk or branch   'o' leaves   ' ' air
  pfn pv_cell(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, u : Int, v : Int, y : Int) : String do
    let g = preview_ground(k)
    let wx = pv_wx(k, cx, cz, seed, u, v)
    let wz = pv_wz(k, cx, cz, seed, u, v)
    let raised = if class_of(k) == cls_field() do pv_field(k, v, g) else shape_at(k, cx, cz, wx, wz, seed, int_to_float(g)) end
    let b = vol_block(k, cx, cz, seed, cell_aux_at(k, cx, cz, seed, g), wx, wz, y, g)
    if b > 0 do (if b == C.crystal() do "*" else if b == C.crystal_beam() do "X" else if b == C.oak_log() do "T" else "o" end end end)
    else if b == 0 do (if y <= g + raised do "." else " " end)
    else if y <= g + raised do (if raised < 0 do "#" else if y <= g do "#" else ":" end end)
    else if y <= g do "."
    else " " end end end end
  end

  pfn pv_row(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, y : Int, i : Int, n : Int, step : Int, across : Bool, acc : String) : String do
    if i > n do acc
    else
      let d = (i - n / 2) * step
      let s = if across do pv_cell(k, cx, cz, seed, 0, d, y) else pv_cell(k, cx, cz, seed, d, 0, y) end
      pv_row(k, cx, cz, seed, y, i + 1, n, step, across, acc ++ s)
    end
  end

  pfn pv_go(k : Int, cx : Int, cz : Int, seed : Int, y : Int, lo : Int, n : Int, step : Int, across : Bool) : Unit do
    if y < lo do ()
    else
      println("   " ++ (if y < 10 do "  " else if y < 100 do " " else "" end end) ++ int_to_string(y) ++ " " ++ pv_row(k, cx, cz, seed, y, 0, n, step, across, ""))
      pv_go(k, cx, cz, seed, y - 1, lo, n, step, across)
    end
  end

  doc "Print kind [k]'s table and two orthographic sections of it standing on flat ground: along its frame and across it."
  fn preview(k : Int, seed : Int) : Unit do
    let cx = 3
    let cz = 5
    let g = preview_ground(k)
    let lo = Math.min_int(Math.min_int(g - 2, vol_y0(k, cx, cz, seed, g) - 2), g - 64)
    -- the top of the drawing: the site's own rise, the volume's top, or an
    -- absolute top level -- the site of an atoll is its lagoon, and asking it
    -- how high the ring is gets a negative number
    let hi = Math.max_int(Math.max_int(g + shape_at(k, cx, cz, sx(k, cx, cz, seed), sz(k, cx, cz, seed), seed, int_to_float(g)) + 2, vol_y1(k, cx, cz, seed, g) + 2),
                          param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_top()) + 4)
    println("poi preview: " ++ kind_name(k) ++ "  (" ++ (if class_of(k) == cls_scatter() do "scatter" else if class_of(k) == cls_field() do "field" else "landmark" end end)
            ++ ", " ++ (if raises(k) do "raises" else "no height" end)
            ++ ", " ++ (if vol_of(k) == vol_none() do "no volume" else if vol_of(k) == vol_carve() do "carves" else "stamps" end end) ++ ")")
    println("  cell " ++ int_to_string(cell(k)) ++ "  density " ++ int_to_string(float_to_int(density(k) *. 100.0)) ++ "%"
            ++ "  country over " ++ int_to_string(float_to_int(cty_lo(k) *. 100.0)) ++ "% at 1/" ++ int_to_string(float_to_int(1.0 /. cty_freq(k))) ++ " blocks"
            ++ "  reach " ++ int_to_string(reach(k)) ++ "  ground >= " ++ int_to_string(min_ground(k)))
    println("  len " ++ int_to_string(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_len())) ++ "  wide " ++ int_to_string(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_wide()))
            ++ "  rise " ++ int_to_string(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_rise())) ++ "  top " ++ int_to_string(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_top()))
            ++ "  depth " ++ int_to_string(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_depth()))
            ++ "  opening " ++ int_to_string(2 * param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_hole_w())) ++ "x" ++ int_to_string(param(k, cx, cz, seed, p_hole_h()))
            ++ "  rules " ++ (if rule_ok(k, cx, cz, seed) do "ok" else "BROKEN" end))
    println("  along the frame (u), one column per 2 blocks:")
    pv_go(k, cx, cz, seed, hi, lo, 40, 2, false)
    println("  across it (v), one column per 2 blocks:")
    pv_go(k, cx, cz, seed, hi, lo, 30, 2, true)
  end

end
